res (e.g., splenomegaly, basophilia, or prior MPN history) rule out CML in blast crisis as a dangerous diagnosis?", "summary": "The absence of chronic features (splenomegaly, basophilia, or prior MPN history) weakens the likelihood of CML in blast crisis (B3.1), though blast crisis can still present acutely without these."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the subacute onset (several days) inconsistent with de novo ALL, which typically presents with rapid progression?", "summary": "Subacute progression is less typical for de novo ALL, which often presents acutely. This weakly argues against B2.1."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset (several days) with constitutional symptoms and blasts in peripheral blood argue against CML in blast crisis, which typically has a more insidious progression?", "summary": "Subacute onset with peripheral blasts is less characteristic of CML in blast crisis, which usually has a more gradual progression. This weakly argues against B3.1."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.56], ["B2.1", 0.374], ["B3", 0.279]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.394], ["B2.1", 0.36], ["B3", 0.157]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway compromise, respiratory failure, shock, uncontrolled hemorrhage) or time-critical syndromes requiring immediate stabilization. Vital signs are stable with no end-organ failure. Blasts in peripheral blood confirm a hematologic emergency (leukemia) but do not necessitate urgent life-saving interventions before diagnostic workup.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "working_differential", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B2.1: ALL) has posterior 0.36 < 0.75, and dangerous myeloid alternatives (B1.1, B1.3) remain unresolved with posterior ≥ 0.10. Residual uncertainty requires further testing to differentiate lymphoid vs. myeloid blast proliferation."}, "turn_bud